  "body": "There's a bug in chromium that images with `loading=lazy` are only lazy-loaded if the URL is \"HTTP family\", which means at least data: and file: URLs are loaded eagerly.\r\n\r\nhttps://crbug.com/1047909\r\n\r\nhttps://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/master:third_party/blink/renderer/core/loader/lazy_image_helper.cc;l=111-112;drc=fd8ec60a5fa61a136eb43f44d381aafd7cce454d?originalUrl=https:%2F%2Fcs.chromium.org%2F\r\n\r\nSince data: URLs are \"loaded\" regardless, I thought that maybe they should be treated as eager even with `loading=lazy` specified (and make it a document conformance error). If one wants to avoid the cost of decoding the image, it's still possible to specify `decoding=async`.\r\n\r\nHowever, special cases can be surprising and annoying for web developers. With `<picture>`, there can be many URLs. You'd have to look at each URL before adding `loading=lazy`.\r\n\r\nHaving written this, I no longer like this idea. I think no URLs should be exempt from `loading=lazy`. :)\r\n\r\nThere should be tests for this, though.\r\n\r\n@domfarolino @emilio ",
